00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:20.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
you were on stage

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.126
for a keynote-- what
was it, day one?

00:06:22.126 --> 00:06:23.500
MARK MANDEL: It
was day one, yes.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.999
FRANCESC CAMPOY: You
did an amazing demo.

00:06:26.999 --> 00:06:28.040
SARA ROBINSON: Thank you.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.690
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Basically everyone told us

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.559
that was, by far, their
favorite part of the whole day.

00:06:34.559 --> 00:06:36.100
So why don't you
tell us a little bit

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.507
about what was it about?

00:06:38.507 --> 00:06:39.340
SARA ROBINSON: Sure.

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.340
I built a demo to showcase the
Video Intelligence API, which

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:46.300
we announced in private
beta on day one of Cloud

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:48.550
Next a couple of weeks ago.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:50.500
What the Video
Intelligence API does

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.320
is it gives you
metadata on videos.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.260
It makes it really easy
to search video content.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:58.650
With the demo I wanted
to show that off.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:02.110
So what the demo was,
I took a lot of videos

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:04.390
and ran them through
the Video API.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:07.660
And the demo let you
visualize all the labels

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.310
it found in a video.

00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:12.400
So it's able to tell you
there is a dog in the video,

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.920
starts at second one and
ends at second three.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:16.790
So on the page for
an individual video,

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:19.120
you'll be able to see
all the granular data

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.025
on what different
labels it found

00:07:21.025 --> 00:07:22.990
at which points in the video.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.750
Then with all that
metadata from the API

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.030
you're also able to
easily implement search

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:30.340
across a large
library of videos.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.190
So I showed how you could
search the library of videos

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:36.070
that I had-- a lot of the videos
were from the Google YouTube

00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:37.210
channel.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.450
So I did a search
of baseball videos.

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:43.420
I wanted to be able to show
what a large media company might

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:45.620
be able to do with the API.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.580
Let's say they had hours and
hours of video footage sitting

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:52.090
in storage buckets Maybe they're
a sports media company that

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.460
has hours of sports
video, but they only

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.482
want to find the content
with a baseball field in it.

00:07:57.482 --> 00:07:59.440
Maybe they're creating
some sort of sizzle reel

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.810
at the end of the week to
highlight, specifically,

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.562
baseball content.

00:08:03.562 --> 00:08:05.020
So if you were to
do this manually,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:06.900
you would have to have
someone on your team

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:08.620
go through all those
hours of footage.

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.560
Watch the video and write down
the times when all the baseball

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:12.640
clips appeared.

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:15.790
With the Video API, with all
the metadata you get back,

00:08:15.790 --> 00:08:18.160
you can pretty easily
implement search

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:20.140
because you're just
searching the JSON that's

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.730
returned from the API.

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:25.240
And it will tell you not only
which videos have exactly

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.490
what you're looking for,
but when in the videos

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.530
your search term appears.

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.020
So the demo was
essentially built

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.883
to showcase those two features.

00:08:34.883 --> 00:08:35.799
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice!

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:37.382
So could you tell
us a little bit more

00:08:37.382 --> 00:08:39.890
about what does it do exactly?

00:08:39.890 --> 00:08:44.169
Because when you tell us about,
oh, it's able to see things,

00:08:44.169 --> 00:08:48.030
like, it's able to recognize
a baseball stadium.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:52.940
I directly think about
the Cloud Vision API.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.720
But when you-- then you say
when in the clip, it makes

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:00.250
me think about a Speech API
that recognizes over time.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:03.912
Where does this fit in between?

00:09:03.912 --> 00:09:04.870
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.550
That's a good question.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.799
So right now, it doesn't
do anything with audio yet.

00:09:09.799 --> 00:09:12.340
But people might see the API
and think, well, isn't this just

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.870
the Vision API taking a frame
and analyzing it and then

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.090
aggregating that content?

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:20.050
So it does do that.

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:23.110
The Video API samples images
at one frame per second,

00:09:23.110 --> 00:09:24.880
and it uses an image
classifier that's

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.650
similar to the Vision API.

00:09:26.650 --> 00:09:29.170
And it does this for
every scene in the video.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:30.910
But the core of the Video API--

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.130
the real magic behind
it-- is that when they're

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.950
done processing a video,
taking all the frames,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.850
they have a separate video
aggregation classifier,

00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:40.900
and then this will
analyze all the features

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:42.292
throughout the video.

00:09:42.292 --> 00:09:43.750
And then at a high
level it will be

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:46.360
able to tell you what
is this video about.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.270
So let's say you had a
video of costumes and candy.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:53.630
It could tell you this is
a video about Halloween.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:55.660
So that aggregation
classifier is really

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:59.180
like the secret sauce
of the Video API.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.540
To build it they
trained this model

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:05.540
on many, many YouTube videos
to be able to do that.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:08.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: When you said
video with costumes and candy,

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:12.490
actually, I thought about
Katy Perry, but that's me.

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:13.740
SARA ROBINSON: That works too.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.530
I don't know if the model
is ready to detect that yet.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So if you
have one frame per second

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:21.710
and you're analyzing
all of those,

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:24.210
there's a bunch of the frames
that are pretty much the same,

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:24.710
right?

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.940
But all of a sudden
there is also

00:10:26.940 --> 00:10:30.760
changes, like, you go from
seeing a house to seeing

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:33.670
a shark dancing, or whatever.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:35.280
Is there something
related to that

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.350
which can allow
you to understand

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.747
the structure of the video

00:10:38.747 --> 00:10:40.080
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.900
There is a feature called
shot change detection.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:45.300
What this does is it's
able to detect every scene

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.270
change in a video.

00:10:46.270 --> 00:10:50.280
So as you said, let's say
you have a vacation video

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:52.830
and you're panning
to some mountains,

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:55.140
and then you move it around
to show some people jumping

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:56.340
up and down.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.950
It's able to detect
whenever the scene changes.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:03.690
And if you request shot change
detection in the annotation

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:06.150
types that you want
back, it will tell you--

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:09.720
it will essentially give you
a JSON object of the start

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:13.950
and end time for every
different scene change

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.180
throughout your video.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.440
So you can do this
to create a highlight

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:20.800
reel of your own video.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:24.420
So that you know exactly
which points in the video

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:25.740
the scene changed.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.600
MARK MANDEL: So you
mentioned something

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.800
in the previous comment you
made which made me think

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.840
of the Vision API as well, which
was that it sounds like you can

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:37.380
pick and choose which features
the Video API actually

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:39.060
goes and finds within it.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.120
I know with a Vision API you
can choose emotion recognition,

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:43.116
or possibly location.

00:11:43.116 --> 00:11:44.490
It sounds like
with the Video API

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.340
you can also do something
similar there too.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.840
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
right now there

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.500
is label detection and
shot change detection.

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.214
But there is definitely more
in the works coming soon

00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:56.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:56.130 --> 00:12:00.180
So what are the things that
you can build with this?

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.662
Because one of the things
that I was imagining is--

00:12:02.662 --> 00:12:04.620
imagine that you have a
very long video, right?

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:08.110
And you're like, oh, I remember
that I was watching this video,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.960
and the last thing
I saw was that scene

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:12.510
where you can see a train.

00:12:12.510 --> 00:12:16.800
So you could say, hey, go to
the scene that has a train.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:18.510
Would that work?

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.410
Is that something that
people are building?

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:21.243
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah.

00:12:21.243 --> 00:12:24.740
I think the biggest use case
for this is searching videos.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.210
Movie navigation like
you just brought up

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.910
is also a good point which
is related to search.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:33.810
So because you get metadata on
what's happening in every scene

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:37.556
it's pretty easy to build an app
that will go to specific scenes

00:12:37.556 --> 00:12:38.430
throughout the video.

00:12:38.430 --> 00:12:40.060
Say you want to
use the shot change

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:44.020
detection to show the frame
at each point in time,

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:45.720
and then go to a
certain frame based

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:50.020
on that image, or a
specific search term.

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.310
So navigation is definitely one,
navigating throughout a movie.

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:55.890
The biggest use case I see
is searching large libraries

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:57.860
of video content.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:00.150
A lot of sites have
a way for users

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:03.090
to upload images and video.

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.220
Or media companies
could have lots

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.880
of video content sitting around
that they need to analyze.

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.310
Or maybe they don't have
any search implemented

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:14.670
on it right now and want to
add search functionality to it.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:16.650
And I can actually talk
about two companies

00:13:16.650 --> 00:13:20.320
that have been using the API to
implement something like this.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.570
So the first one
is called Cantemo,

00:13:22.570 --> 00:13:26.040
and they are a media
asset management company.

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:28.320
What they do-- their
customers are anyone

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:29.550
with a lot of video content.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:31.110
Could be an individual
or a company

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:32.760
that has a lot of videos.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.640
And they make it
easy to, essentially,

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:37.690
manage your video library.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:39.600
So when you upload a
video to their service

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:41.580
they'll transcode it for you.

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.610
And they're using the Video
API to improve their search.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.580
So this way they can make
it so customers can easily

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.660
search for, like you said,
find all my videos of the train

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:56.200
and it'll know exactly at which
point in the video that occurs.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:14:00.600
So that's one example of a media
company using the Video API.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.510
Another company is Wix.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:06.450
Wix is a service, for those
of you that don't know,

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:10.290
that lets anybody
create a web site.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:11.790
And you don't have
to be a developer

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:13.800
to be able to create
a website on Wix.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.750
They're using the Video API
for what they call Wix Media

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:21.840
Platform, which is a platform
that they use to support users

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.550
that are uploading
a lot of images

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.530
and video files every day.

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:29.830
So when a user uploads a video,
or someone is building a web

00:14:29.830 --> 00:14:32.670
site and one of their
users uploads a video,

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.800
the Wix Media Platform
will transcode it.

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.611
Then they'll use the Video
API, along with shot change

00:14:37.611 --> 00:14:40.110
detection-- so they use both
shot change detection and label

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:41.370
detection--

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:43.380
to grab the main
images from the video

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:46.620
so that they can show users
the highlights of what

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:48.840
is this video about.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:50.640
They also use the
metadata returned

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:53.550
to add tags to their videos,
and then with this they're

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:56.700
also able to add search.

00:14:56.700 --> 00:15:01.900
So if they've got a website that
has a lot of user video content

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:04.880
it makes it easy for users to
search, both within a video

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.582
and in an entire
library of videos.

00:15:07.582 --> 00:15:09.290
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
was wondering if there

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:12.260
is some of the features
that we've seen

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:16.550
and we've used on Cloud Vision
API, if those are available

00:15:16.550 --> 00:15:19.160
In Cloud Video Intelligence API.

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:22.310
Specifically, I'm thinking
about that amazing demo

00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:25.580
that we had a Google IO
and, I think, GCP Next too,

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:28.220
which was the Emotobooth.

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.140
You remember that it
takes a picture of you

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:32.630
and according to the reaction
of your face, the feeling

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:35.390
that-- if you are smiling
or you have anger,

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.060
it makes the background
a little bit different.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.760
I was wondering if the
Cloud Video Intelligence

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:44.180
API allows you to do
things like, hey, find me

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.530
all the faces in a video.

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:47.349
Or find a face that is smiling.

00:15:47.349 --> 00:15:48.140
This kind of thing.

00:15:48.140 --> 00:15:49.985
Is this something that
is already supported?

00:15:49.985 --> 00:15:52.610
SARA ROBINSON: So right now only
label protection is supported,

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:54.360
but that's definitely
something that we've

00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:55.820
gotten a lot of feedback on.

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:59.990
And the team is working on
adding more of those features

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:01.700
into the Video API.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.670
So one thing that was really
popular at the Vision API

00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:06.760
is the inappropriate
content detection.

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:08.510
So you pass it an
image, it's able to tell

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:10.880
you is this image appropriate
or not, which eliminates

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:14.030
the need for a lot of manual
classification on images

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:16.400
if you've got a site
where users are uploading

00:16:16.400 --> 00:16:18.380
a ton of images every day.

00:16:18.380 --> 00:16:20.760
Instead of having somebody
manually flag those images

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.330
you can use the Vision
API safe search detection

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:24.350
method to do that.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:27.620
That is something that obviously
has a big use case for videos

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:31.670
as well, and we're working
on adding more features to it

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:32.180
soon.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:35.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I mean, I guess
for any of those you could also

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:38.060
use the shot frame detection,
and go find one of the frames

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:41.300
and use that as the input
to the Cloud Vision API

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:42.230
and play with that.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:43.850
Is that something
that you have tried?

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:44.810
SARA ROBINSON: I haven't
tried that personally,

00:16:44.810 --> 00:16:47.530
but that's definitely something
you could do right now.

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:49.160
So if there was a
specific label that

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:51.440
was returned from
your video, you

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.210
would know exactly
which frame to go

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:54.750
so you can grab that frame.

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:57.090
The times it returns
are in microseconds,

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.450
so you could grab that
exact frame that you wanted

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:03.170
and send it to the Vision
API to run a face detection

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:06.557
or safe search detection on it
to get more data on your video.

00:17:06.557 --> 00:17:08.390
MARK MANDEL: I've been
thinking about trying

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:09.764
to run it through
security camera

00:17:09.764 --> 00:17:11.681
footage, like, certain
stuff outside my house.

00:17:11.681 --> 00:17:13.180
Be like, show me
all the times there

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:15.079
was a person sitting
at my front doorstep.

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:16.130
SARA ROBINSON: That would
be a great use case.

00:17:16.130 --> 00:17:17.960
And the API right
now can probably

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:21.048
recognize that there there's
a person in the scene.

00:17:21.048 --> 00:17:23.089
So if you find the scene
that has a person in it,

00:17:23.089 --> 00:17:25.460
you can go ahead and send
that to the Vision API

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.640
to get more data on is this
person happy, or angry,

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:29.445
or surprised.

00:17:29.445 --> 00:17:31.070
MARK MANDEL: Or maybe
catch the coyotes

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:33.260
that have been roaming
my neighborhood recently.

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:34.540
SARA ROBINSON: Exactly.

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:37.310
Figure out exactly what
times they go by your house.

00:17:37.310 --> 00:17:40.007
MARK MANDEL: So let's talk about
the technology behind this.

00:17:40.007 --> 00:17:41.090
Can you talk a bit about--

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:42.935
I know you mentioned the
YouTube dataset-- but can

00:17:42.935 --> 00:17:44.480
you talk about
what's powering this?

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.250
Is this another
TensorFlow project?

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.625
SARA ROBINSON: So there's a
couple of models powering it.

00:17:48.625 --> 00:17:51.620
They use the YouTube videos to
train their video aggregation

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:53.360
classifier.

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:56.010
And then it samples
images, I mentioned before,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.320
at one frame per second.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:00.140
To do this it's using a
custom image classifier,

00:18:00.140 --> 00:18:01.599
which is similar
to the Vision API,

00:18:01.599 --> 00:18:02.723
but a little bit different.

00:18:02.723 --> 00:18:04.970
And that's what it's using
to go through the video

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:07.770
and grab an image
from every scene.

00:18:07.770 --> 00:18:09.470
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
one of the things

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:11.780
that I really like about
these kind of APIs--

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:16.280
specifically Cloud Vision
API, Video Intelligence API,

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:18.180
and also speech
APIs-- is that you

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.690
can start building
really cool demos that

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:21.590
are very interactive.

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:24.980
So I feel like lots of people
that are not necessarily

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:28.880
deeply technical could take
some benefits out of this

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:30.680
and create very artsy demos.

00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.750
Is this easy to use?

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:33.800
How does it work?

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:34.640
SARA ROBINSON: Definitely, yeah.

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:35.570
It's very easy to use.

00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:37.195
One thing that I love
about our machine

00:18:37.195 --> 00:18:39.050
learning APIs is
that they give you

00:18:39.050 --> 00:18:41.600
access to a pre-trained
machine learning model

00:18:41.600 --> 00:18:43.620
with just one REST API request.

00:18:43.620 --> 00:18:45.907
So I called them our
friendly machine learning.

00:18:45.907 --> 00:18:47.990
If you don't have much of
machine learning or data

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:50.350
science background these
are really accessible.

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.480
All you need to know how to
do is make a REST API request

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:56.340
and you get access to all of
this machine learning data.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.680
The video API is
very easy to use.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:02.120
All you need to do is pass
it the URL of your video

00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:05.284
in Google Cloud Storage, which
is our object storage tool.

00:19:05.284 --> 00:19:07.700
So right now that's what's
supported for making a request.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:11.120
So once you get your video in
cloud storage you send that

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:13.580
video URL and your
API request, you also

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:16.250
tell it which types of
detection you want to do.

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:18.620
Right now you should do either
label detection or a shot

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:22.220
change detection, and that
will kick off an operation.

00:19:22.220 --> 00:19:25.010
One thing you can also
pass into your request

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:28.950
is an output URL for the JSON
to be sent in cloud storage.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:30.770
So if you want to
store the annotation

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:32.930
JSON in cloud
storage, you can just

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.380
pass that URL into your request.

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:37.460
And then when the Video
API is done processing it

00:19:37.460 --> 00:19:39.480
will write it to cloud storage.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:40.299
If you want the--

00:19:40.299 --> 00:19:41.840
Yeah, that's a really
useful feature.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.930
I actually used that for
the demo that I built.

00:19:44.930 --> 00:19:47.600
If you want the annotations
right away, all you need to do

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:51.350
is just ping the operation ID
and see what the status is.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:54.560
It will give you a progress
on how far along it is.

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:58.100
Once it's done it will return
the annotations to you.

00:19:58.100 --> 00:20:00.840
MARK MANDEL: I think that
would be a neat way integrating

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.090
with Firebase Storage as well.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.173
SARA ROBINSON: Definitely.

00:20:03.173 --> 00:20:06.060
I didn't use Firebase for this,
but I used Cloud Functions

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:06.930
to build the demo.

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:09.330
So what I did was I had
a Google Cloud storage

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:12.630
bucket that had all my videos,
and I had a class function

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:13.720
listening on that bucket.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:15.300
So whenever a new
file is added it

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:17.380
checked if it was a video file.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:19.200
If it was, it would
send it to the API.

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.200
And because I didn't need
annotations right away

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:23.880
I just had it output the results
to a separate cloud storage

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:26.610
bucket with the annotation JSON.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:29.170
Then I used Node on App
Engine for the front end.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:31.560
So I wasn't calling
the Video API directly

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:33.960
from the App Engine
app, all it did

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.140
was grab all the videos
from the cloud storage

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:39.420
bucket and their associated
metadata from the other bucket.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:42.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So this
gave me two questions.

00:20:42.460 --> 00:20:45.407
The first one, is
that code open-source?

00:20:45.407 --> 00:20:46.990
SARA ROBINSON: That's
a good question.

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:51.160
The answer is yes,
hopefully soon.

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:53.190
I'm starting on that
process this week.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:55.320
So once the code
is open source I'm

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:57.150
hoping to do a
blog post about it,

00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:59.702
and I'll give you a link to
add to the show notes as well.

00:20:59.702 --> 00:21:00.660
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:03.259
I expect by the time this comes
out you should have it done.

00:21:03.259 --> 00:21:04.800
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
that's the goal.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:08.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The second
question is, how fast is this?

00:21:08.310 --> 00:21:13.260
Like you said, you can give
the URL for a YouTube video,

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:16.470
let's say, a video on
Google Cloud Storage.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.900
Let's say that the
video is one minute.

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:22.020
How long should I
expect this to take?

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:22.680
Seconds?

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:23.561
Micro-seconds?

00:21:23.561 --> 00:21:24.060
Minutes?

00:21:24.060 --> 00:21:24.750
Hours?

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:26.310
SARA ROBINSON: Right now
it takes about one fourth

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:27.351
of the time of the video.

00:21:27.351 --> 00:21:30.210
So for your one-minute video
it would take about 15 seconds

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:33.750
to run the annotations, which
is actually pretty fast.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.980
So it's not real time, but you
can get the annotation results

00:21:37.980 --> 00:21:38.880
pretty fast.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:40.530
What would take
a while is if you

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:44.770
had a large library of videos,
maybe terabytes of videos,

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:47.370
it could take a matter
of days to process.

00:21:47.370 --> 00:21:49.750
But right now I've seen it
about one fourth the length

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:50.424
of the video.

00:21:50.424 --> 00:21:51.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

