MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:41.145
All right, why
don't we get stuck

00:25:41.145 --> 00:25:43.340
into our question of the week.

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:46.190
So the situation is thusly--

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:49.497
say I've got one or
more virtual machines,

00:25:49.497 --> 00:25:51.830
they have some data on them
and some sort of application

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:52.830
running on them as well.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.800
And say the data on
those is going to change.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:56.251
The classic example--
which people

00:25:56.251 --> 00:25:57.750
don't do probably
as much any more--

00:25:57.750 --> 00:26:00.680
is something like an FTP
server or something similar.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.060
That kind of scenario.

00:26:02.060 --> 00:26:04.490
What are my options,
or the best practices

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:07.066
I should use for making
sure that data is backed up?

00:26:07.066 --> 00:26:09.440
Because I want to make sure
that if something bad happens

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:12.470
to that VM or the disk gets
corrupted in some crazy way,

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:14.240
which shouldn't
happen but who knows,

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:16.430
what can I use to make
sure that I can recover

00:26:16.430 --> 00:26:17.950
from that relatively quickly?

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:22.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
question comes from FRWillia23.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:23.430
Free Willy, maybe?

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:24.999
I don't know.

00:26:24.999 --> 00:26:27.040
I think that it is pretty
interesting because you

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:30.350
were saying in case the disk
gets a corruption or something,

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:31.830
that will never happen.

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:34.150
But your software could
have a bug, right,

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:38.720
so you could somehow wipe all
the data on your hard disk.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:41.210
This is something that
happens, and this is something

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:42.680
that I've personally done.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:43.610
It is not fun.

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:47.150
So you want to make sure
that you keep copies.

00:26:47.150 --> 00:26:50.660
The question has a very
obvious answer, which

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:53.300
is, just use disk snapshots.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:55.190
Because they're really
made for that, right?

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:57.140
I thought that was
probably the only answer,

00:26:57.140 --> 00:26:59.790
but we also started
chatting about, well,

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:03.917
could we do the same thing
with Google Cloud Storage?

00:27:03.917 --> 00:27:05.750
Especially because with
Google Cloud Storage

00:27:05.750 --> 00:27:07.730
you have Nearline
and Coldline, which

00:27:07.730 --> 00:27:09.890
are classes of
storage that allow

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.860
you to store the same data
with the same durability.

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:15.470
But with less-- basically,
it takes a little bit

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:18.470
longer to access,
but it's way cheaper.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:20.540
So we're just
wondering, oh, it's

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:22.100
probably the best solution.

00:27:22.100 --> 00:27:24.350
Turns out that it is not.

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:27.620
Because if you're
running snapshots,

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:30.230
the price for
snapshots or Nearline

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:33.710
is the same, exactly the same.

00:27:33.710 --> 00:27:36.060
With Coldline you could
go a little bit cheaper.

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:39.900
But then you have the problem
that, for Nearline or Coldline,

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:41.660
creating a snapshot
from that data

00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:44.630
is not as simple as creating
a snapshot from a snapshot.

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:46.130
Because when you
have a snapshot,

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:48.850
everything you need to do if
you want to create a new disk

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:50.960
is just one click
and you're done.

00:27:50.960 --> 00:27:52.040
Or one REST API.

00:27:52.040 --> 00:27:53.790
Or one gcloud command.

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:56.480
So it is way simpler.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:59.750
And unless you have
a huge amount of data

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:02.600
that you were storing in--

00:28:02.600 --> 00:28:04.460
you were backing up--

00:28:04.460 --> 00:28:07.010
and then in that case,
as you were mentioning,

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:09.650
maybe you should be
using persistent disks.

00:28:09.650 --> 00:28:12.560
So unless you're using
a huge amount of data

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:16.040
on persistent disks and that
little difference in price

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:20.180
makes a lot of difference,
I'd say that you should just

00:28:20.180 --> 00:28:21.260
simply use snapshots.

00:28:21.260 --> 00:28:23.660
Because once the
price is [INAUDIBLE]--

00:28:23.660 --> 00:28:26.510
it's actually the
same as Nearline,

00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:28.430
and they're much easier to use

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.090
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it's
worth noting that snapshots

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:32.994
are diff based,
so you don't have

00:28:32.994 --> 00:28:35.410
to grab the whole thing again,
which you might necessarily

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.701
need to do if you're going
to shift all your stuff over

00:28:37.701 --> 00:28:39.812
to some sort of cloud
storage instance.

00:28:39.812 --> 00:28:41.270
What's also kind
of cool about them

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:44.310
is that snapshots are
globally available.

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:46.610
So if you want to
restore them, you

00:28:46.610 --> 00:28:50.420
can restore them to any
zone within your project.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.170
So that makes them
super, super useful,

00:28:52.170 --> 00:28:54.503
especially if you want to
capture an operating system as

00:28:54.503 --> 00:28:56.275
well as your data
at the same time.

00:28:56.275 --> 00:28:58.200
So your recovery
time is pretty sweet.

00:28:58.200 --> 00:28:58.698
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
think that in that case

00:28:58.698 --> 00:29:01.040
it is important to take
into account that when

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:04.480
you have a snapshot
and you're copying it

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:08.090
across networks,
across regions, there's

00:29:08.090 --> 00:29:10.370
a fee associated to that.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:12.470
Because you're actually
transferring data

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:15.320
across networks, so there's like
ingress and egress and stuff

00:29:15.320 --> 00:29:17.090
that you take into account.

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:19.910
Very related to that, if
you store that information

00:29:19.910 --> 00:29:22.670
into cloud storage, into
Nearline or Coldline,

00:29:22.670 --> 00:29:26.040
you also have a cost associated
to reading all that data.

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:28.760
So we have a bunch of
links in the show notes.

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.890
There's actually a
very good article

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:33.950
that we found while doing the
research for this question,

00:29:33.950 --> 00:29:38.180
which the name is "Designing
a Disaster Recovery Plan."

00:29:38.180 --> 00:29:40.790
It covers instant snapshots
and way more than that.

00:29:40.790 --> 00:29:45.650
So maybe even a good
topic for an episode.

00:29:45.650 --> 00:29:47.760
I don't know who would
